      "text": "To solve this problem, we will first determine all possible outcomes when two 6-sided dice are tossed. There are a total of \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\) possible outcomes. \n\nHere is the Python code to identify and count the favorable outcomes where the sum of the numbers on the dice is a prime number:\n\n```python\nimport sympy\n\ndice_faces = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\n\n# List all possible sums of two dice\npossible_sums = [a + b for a in dice_faces for b in dice_faces]\n\n# Filter the sums that are prime\nprime_sums = [x for x in possible_sums if sympy.isprime(x)]\n\n# The number of ways to get each prime sum\nprime_count = len(prime_sums)\nprime_possible_sums = set(prime_sums)\n\n# Total number of possible outcomes\ntotal_outcomes = len(possible_sums)\nfavourable_outcomes = len(prime_possible_sums)\n\n# The probability\nprobability = favourable_outcomes / total_outcomes\n\nprint(probability)\n```\n\n**Rerunning the given code snippet:**\n\n1. The Python code builds a set of all possible sums for two dice falling. It also generates a list of these sums.\n2. The code then filters out only those numbers that are prime using the sympy library.\n3. The outputs repository now indicates that the total possible outcomes for the sum of the numbers thrown are 36.\n\n**Mathematical Steps to determine the favorable outcomes:**\n\n**Possible sums of two dice:**\n\n1. 1 + 1 = 2\n2. 1 + 2 = 3\n3. 1 + 3 = 4\n4. 1 + 4 = 5\n5. 1 + 5 = 6\n6. 1 + 6 = 7\n7. 2 + 1 = 3\n8. 2 + 2 = 4\n9. 2 + 3 = 5\n10. 2 + 4 = 6\n11. 2 + 5 = 7\n12. 2 + 6 = 8\n13. 3 + 1 = 4\n14. 3 + 2 = 5\n15. 3 + 3 = 6\n16. 3 + 4 = 7\n17. 3 + 5 = 8\n18. 3 + 6 = 9\n19. 4 + 1 = 5\n20. 4 + 2 = 6\n21. 4 + 3 = 7\n22. 4 + 4 = 8\n23. 4 + 5 = 9\n24. 4 + 6 = 10\n25. 5 + 1 = 6\n26. 5 + 2 = 7\n27. 5 + 3 = 8\n28. 5 + 4 = 9\n29. 5 + 5 = 10\n30. 5 + 6 = 11\n31. 6 + 1 = 7\n32. 6 + 2 = 8\n33. 6 + 3 = 9\n34. 6 + 4 = 10\n35. 6 + 5 = 11\n36. 6 + 6 = 12\n\n2. Prime sums:\n   2, 3, 5, 7, 11\n\n• Checking for each number if it is a prime number:\n   2: 2, 5, 11 (3 ways)\n   3: 3, 4, 11, 13 (4 ways)\n   5: 5, 6, 10, 13, 17 (5 ways)\n   7: 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13, 16 (8 ways)\n   11: 5, 6, 8, 9, 12, 14, 16, 18, 22 (10 ways)\n\n   Summing them up gives 3 + 4 + 5 + 8 + 10 = 30 ways.\n\n**The numbers that have been counted twice (including the prime counts):**\n\n• 3-4\n• 4-1\n• 5-2\n• 6-3\n• 8-4\n• 9-5\n• 10-6\n• 11-7\n\n3. Filtering the numbers based on these sets:\n   Only distinct numbers could be counted:\n   2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11, 13\n\n4. 2 occurs twice and 13 occurs twice:\n\n• So the total number is 15.\n\n**By incorporating the alternate method, one concludes that the prime sum—`\n\nThe provided solution is incorrect. The correct steps are:\n\n1. Identify all possible sums of rolling two 6-sided dice, which are \\(2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 9, 9, 10, 10, 11, 11, 12\\).\n\n2. Filter only the sums that are prime numbers: \\(2, 3, 5, 7, 11\\).\n\n3. Count the favorable outcomes: There are 5 prime sums.\n\n4. Calculate the probability:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total possible outcomes}} = \\frac{5}{36}\n   \\]\n\nWe don't need the sympy module for this simple task. Let's write the corrected Python code to verify:\n\n```python\npossible_outcomes = [2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 9, 9, 10, 10, 11, 11, 12]\nprime_sums = [x for x in possible_outcomes if x in [2, 3, 5, 7, 11]]\n\nfavorable_outcomes = len(prime_sums)\ntotal_outcomes = len(possible_outcomes)\nprobability = favorable_outcomes / total_outcomes\n\nprint(probability)\n```",
